Short:        Fetch GitHub releases, ReAction GUI
Author:       Mathieu Burgerhout
Uploader:     Mathieu Burgerhout
Type:         comm/tcp
Version:      0.1
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.5
Requires:     OS 3.5+ (ReAction), 68020, 640x400 screen, TCP/IP stack,
              AmiSSL 5, correctly set system clock
Distribution: Aminet

Nearly all new Amiga software is published through GitHub Releases, which
is awkward on an Amiga: no browser for the platform can handle the CSS and
JavaScript the site relies on. Getting to the newest .lha usually means
walking over to a modern machine.

GitFetch does that part for you. Type a GitHub address or owner/repo, pick
a release and a file, and it lands on your hard drive.

Features:

  - ReAction interface; runs on a plain 640x256 PAL screen
  - Saved repositories, kept in ENVARC: as plain text
  - Choose how many releases to fetch, to keep traffic down
  - Asks before overwriting an existing file, and warns when a volume
    looks too full
  - Downloads run in their own process, so the window stays responsive
    and Stop works even during a slow transfer
  - Warns about a wrongly set clock before TLS fails over it

Two ways to reach GitHub:

  - Directly, using AmiSSL 5. No server of your own needed, but GitHub
    sends its full reply (about 240 KB for 25 releases) and every byte
    passes through TLS decryption on a 68k.

  - Through a small proxy of your own. It turns the same reply into about
    5 KB of plain text, already in the Amiga character set, and caches it
    so GitHub's limit of 60 requests per hour is not an issue. The proxy
    is a Python script using only the standard library and is included.

It downloads, and nothing else; unpacking and installing stay in your own
hands.

Requirements:

  - AmigaOS 3.5 or newer, for the ReAction classes
  - 68020 or better
  - A screen of 640x400 or larger; smaller gets too cramped
  - A TCP/IP stack: Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami or Genesis
  - AmiSSL 5 or newer, from Aminet
  - A correctly set system clock

That last one is easy to overlook and worth spelling out. An Amiga without
a working battery-backed clock starts in 1978, and every TLS certificate
then looks "not yet valid", so a direct connection to GitHub fails. The
error OpenSSL reports says nothing about dates, which makes it a puzzling
one to run into.

GitFetch checks the date before connecting and tells you plainly if it is
wrong. It does not set the clock itself: that is a job for a proper time
program such as the NTP client built into Roadshow, or SNTP from Aminet.

To check and correct by hand:

    date
    date 21-aug-26 17:00:00

If you would rather not deal with AmiSSL or the clock at all, the included
proxy is an alternative: it needs no TLS on the Amiga side and no correct
clock, only a machine of your own to run it on.

Full documentation is in the included AmigaGuide file.

Source and new versions:
https://github.com/MathieuSEO/GitFetch

GitFetch can fetch its own updates: the repository is in the saved list
from the start.

